STRINGTABLE DISCARDABLE
BEGIN
    8165                            "<color 135 181 255>Basics: Help Files@<color 200 241 255>Help Files\n<color 135 181 255>Help files are brief descriptions of common objects encountered in the Tron universe. When a new help file has been added, an icon with a '?' will appear on the left-hand side of the screen.\n\nWhile in the Help File Browser, help file topics are listed in the order acquired. White indicates the currently selected help file, yellow indicates a new help file that has yet to be opened and black indicates a previously viewed help file.\n\nTo recall a help file at any time, press the 'Browse Help Files' shortcut key <color 255 255 0>(Default Key: 8)<color 135 181 255>. To close a help file, press 'Esc' or the 'Action' key <color 255 255 0>(Default Key: F)<color 135 181 255>."
    8166                            "<color 135 181 255>Basics: Talk Icon@<color 200 241 255>Talk Icon\n<color 135 181 255>A yellow talk icon will appear over a Program's head when he or she has useful information.\n\nPress the 'Action' key <color 255 255 0>(Default Key: F)<color 135 181 255> to initiate the conversation."
    8167                            "<color 135 181 255>Basics: Player Movement@<color 200 241 255>Default Player Movement Keys\n\n<color 135 181 255>Forward = <color 255 255 0>W<color 135 181 255> \nBackward = <color 255 255 0>S<color 135 181 255> \nStrafe left = <color 255 255 0>A<color 135 181 255> \nStrafe right = <color 255 255 0>D<color 135 181 255> \nCrouch - <color 255 255 0>C<color 135 181 255> \nJump = <color 255 255 0>Space bar<color 135 181 255> \nLean left = <color 255 255 0>Q <color 135 181 255> \nLean right = <color 255 255 0>E<color 135 181 255> \nWalk = <color 255 255 0>Shift<color 135 181 255>\n\nEnemies will react to any sound that is made. Cautious movement (walking and crouching) will generate less of a disturbance. It is possible to lean around corners and safely observe what lies ahead. Be careful though, enemies will eventually detect your presence if you lean too long."
    8168                            "<color 135 181 255>Basics: Status Display@<color 200 241 255>Status Display\n<color 135 181 255>When you have a new objective or task, the 'Objectives Updated' icon located on the left-hand side of the screen will appear. Press the 'Status Display' key <color 255 255 0>(Default Key: Tab)<color 135 181 255> to display your current objective and tasks.\n\nThe over-arching objective is listed at the top of the status display. Immediately below it, are the individual tasks that must be performed to complete the objective. Once a task or an objective is complete, it is removed from the list."
    8169                            "<color 135 181 255>Basics: Build Points@<color 200 241 255>Build Points\n<color 135 181 255>Build points are awarded for completing mission tasks. Acquiring build points increases your version number that is displayed in the upper left-hand corner of the screen. When you collect 100 build points, the first digit in your version number will increase (e.g. v1.0.0), indicating that you have reached a new milestone. Reaching a new milestone will allow you to increase your performance ratings.\n\n Press the <color 255 255 0>F1 key<color 135 181 255> to manage your performance ratings."
    8170                            "<color 135 181 255>Basics: Build Notes@<color 200 241 255>Build Notes\n<color 135 181 255>In addition to completing a mission task, picking up a build note will also increase your build point total. Each level contains a finite quantity of build notes. A counter in the upper left hand corner displays the total build notes acquired versus the total build notes available in each level.\n\nThere are a total of one hundred build notes available in the game. A global build note counter can be found in the subroutine menu.\n\nTo pick up a build note, face the build note while near it and press the 'Action' key <color 255 255 0>(Default Key: F.)"
    8171                            "<color 135 181 255>Basics: I/O Nodes@<color 200 241 255>I/O Nodes\n<color 135 181 255>I/O nodes are used to receive information from other programs not in your vicinity. When a non-local program wants to communicate with you, its icon will appear on the left-hand side of the screen and the nearest I/O Node will brighten.\n\nTo activate an I/O Node, face it and press the 'Action' key<color 255 255 0> (Default Key: F.)"
    8172                            "<color 135 181 255>Basics: Permissions@<color 200 241 255>Permissions\n<color 135 181 255>A segmented ring <color 255 255 0>(Figure 1)<color 135 181 255> in the center of your screen will indicate which 'Permissions' an object requires to be activated. Missing permissions are marked as red and permissions that you already have are marked as light blue. Your complete inventory of permissions, called a 'Permission Set', is displayed as light blue on your lower HUD display <color 255 255 0>(Figure 2)<color 135 181 255>."
    8173                            "<color 135 181 255>Basics: Archive Bins@<color 200 241 255>Archive Bins\n<color 135 181 255>Archive bins are storage devices that contain downloadable items such as permissions, subroutines and e-mails. Most items require energy to download.\n\nItems nodes can help inform you of an archive bin's contents without accessing them.<color 255 0 0> Red nodes<color 135 181 255> represent subroutines,<color 0 255 0> Green nodes<color 135 181 255> represent emails and<color 0 0 255> Blue nodes<color 135 181 255> represent permissions. If no item nodes are present, the archive bin is empty.\n\nPress the 'Action' key <color 255 255 0>(Default Key: F)<color 135 181 255> to activate the directory of stored items. To download an item, press the corresponding number on your keyboard."
    8174                            "<color 135 181 255>Basics: Binary Bits@<color 200 241 255>Binary Bits\n<color 135 181 255>Binary Bits are used to operate socket switches. To activate a socket switch, face the binary bit while near it and press the 'Action' key <color 255 255 0>(Default Key: F.) <color 135 181 255>\n\nIt is common to find a socket missing its binary bit. Look around. There is a good chance a powered down binary bit is located nearby. To transfer energy to a powered down binary bit, press and hold the 'Action' key <color 255 255 0>(Default Key: F) <color 135 181 255> until the bit comes to life (also indicated when the colored meter on the screen fills up.)"
END

STRINGTABLE DISCARDABLE
BEGIN
    8176                            "<color 135 181 255>Basics: Data Streams@<color 200 241 255>Data Streams\n<color 135 181 255>Data streams <color 255 255 0>(Figure 1)<color 135 181 255> are used to transport programs locally within the digital world. Facing a data stream will display its destination on the center of the screen as well as whether it is a one-way or two-way portal. Once entering a one-way data stream, it will be impossible to return, so be prepared."
    8177                            "<color 135 181 255>Basics: Patch Routines@<color 200 241 255>Patch Routines\n<color 135 181 255>Patch routines will provide you with health and energy. To activate, press and hold the 'Action' key <color 255 255 0>(Default Key: F)<color 135 181 255>.\n\n<color 255 255 255>Health Patch Routines <color 255 255 0>(Figure 1)<color 135 181 255> will replenish you with a limited amount of health.\n\n<color 255 255 255>Energy Patch Routines <color 255 255 0>(Figure 2)<color 135 181 255> will replenish you with a limited amount of energy.\n\n<color 255 255 255>Infinite Patch Routines <color 255 255 0>(Figure 3)<color 135 181 255> contain an infinite supply of energy."
    8178                            "<color 135 181 255>Basics: Save / Load@<color 200 241 255>Save / Load\n<color 135 181 255>It is highly recommended to save your game often.\n\nTo <color 255 255 255>QUICK SAVE<color 135 181 255> your game at anytime (with the exception of when you are racing light cycles) press the <color 255 255 0>F5 key<color 135 181 255>\n\nTo <color 255 255 255>QUICK LOAD<color 135 181 255> your <color 255 255 255>QUICK SAVE<color 135 181 255>, press the <color 255 255 0>F9 key.<color 135 181 255>\n\nIf you want to save multiple games, press the Esc key to access the 'Save Game' and 'Load Game' menus."
    8179                            "<color 135 181 255>Weapons: Disc Primitive@<color 200 241 255>Disc Primitive\n\n<color 255 255 255>Auto Guidance:<color 135 181 255> By quickly pressing and releasing the 'Fire' key <color 255 255 0>(Default Key: Left mouse button)<color 135 181 255>, the disc will be automatically guided towards the enemy target.\n\n<color 255 255 255>Manual Guidance:<color 135 181 255> Manual control of the disc is achieved by pressing and holding the 'Fire' key while steering it with the mouse.\n\n<color 255 255 255>Recall and Block:<color 135 181 255> To recall the disc or block enemy discs, press the 'Disc recall / block' key <color 255 255 0>(Default Key: Right mouse button)<color 135 181 255>.\n\n<color 255 255 255>Melee: <color 135 181 255> Finally, by pressing the 'Disc attack modifier' key <color 255 255 0>(Default Key: Ctrl)<color 135 181 255> while pressing the 'Fire' key, you can perform a disc melee attack."
    8180                            "<color 135 181 255>Basics: Core Dumps@<color 200 241 255>Core Dumps\n<color 135 181 255>When enemy programs are de-rezzed, they leave behind core dumps. Core dumps contain energy, health and often permissions and/or subroutines. The energy and health available in each core dump will diminish over time until it eventually reaches zero causing it to disappear. Retrieving a core dump immediately after defeating an enemy will award the greatest benefit.\n\nCore dumps that contain inventory other than energy and health exhibit small pyramids inside the glowing orb.\n\nTo pick up a core dump, face it and press the 'Action' key <color 255 255 0>(Default Key: F)."
    8181                            "<color 135 181 255>Basics: Corruption@<color 200 241 255>Corruption\n<color 255 255 0>IMPORTANT<color 135 181 255>:During your missions you will encounter enemy programs who may corrupt your system memory. When an installed subroutine becomes corrupted <color 255 255 0>(Figure 1)<color 135 181 255>, the active subroutine icon on your display will turn green <color 255 255 0>(Figure 2)<color 135 181 255>. When corrupted, your subroutine will either be less effective or not work as intended.\n\nYour system memory may also fill up with bad blocks <color 255 255 0>(Figure 3)<color 135 181 255> when encountering corrupted programs. A bad black in the system memory makes that block of memory unusable by a subroutine.\n\nTo clean a corrupted subroutine or remove a bad block, use the <color 255 255 255>DISINFECT<color 135 181 255> and <color 255 255 255>DEFRAG<color 135 181 255> procedurals in the subroutine menu (see Procedural Help File.)"
    8182                            "<color 135 181 255>Basics: Subroutines@<color 200 241 255>Subroutines\n<color 135 181 255>A subroutine is a self-contained little program that can be installed into your system memory.\n\nThere are three families of subroutines:\n<color 255 255 255>Combat Subroutines<color 135 181 255> can modify Primitives into a wide range of unique weapons.\n\n<color 255 255 255>Defense Subroutines<color 135 181 255> can add varying degrees of armor protection.\n\n<color 255 255 255>Utility Subroutines<color 135 181 255> give you additional capabilities. For example, the 'Y-amp' <color 255 255 0>(Figure 1)<color 135 181 255> is a utility subroutine that will enable you to jump higher."
    8183                            "<color 135 181 255>Basics: Installing Subroutines@<color 200 241 255>Installing Subroutines\n<color 135 181 255>To install a subroutine into your system memory, click on the subroutine with the left mouse button and drop it into an empty slot with another mouse click <color 255 255 0>(Figure 1 and 2)<color 135 181 255>. Once it is installed, press F1 to enable the subroutine.\n\nTip: To quickly install or uninstall a subroutine, right click on the subroutine.\n\nNote: The space available in system memory is unique to each system environment. Powerful systems will have the most segments available to install subroutines while low-end systems will have the least amount of free segments. Prior to entering a new system, you will be asked to reconfigure your subroutines."
    8184                            "<color 135 181 255>Basics: Code Optimization Ware@<color 200 241 255>Code Optimization Ware\n<color 135 181 255>A Code Optimization Ware is a device that scans the digital universe looking for code to optimize (i.e. make more efficient). Code Optimization Wares can also be used to optimize your subroutines, increasing their effectiveness and making them smaller; thus, allowing you to store more subroutines in your system memory.\n\nTo ready a Code Optimization Ware for optimizing, approach it then press the 'Action' key <color 255 255 0>(Default Key: F.)"
    8185                            "<color 135 181 255>Basics: Optimizing Subroutines@<color 200 241 255>Optimizing Subroutines\n<color 135 181 255>Subroutines have three different optimization states: Alpha, Beta, and Gold.\n\n<color 255 255 255>ALPHA<color 135 181 255> subroutines are the least efficient of the three states. They take up three slots of system memory and provide basic functionality.\n\n<color 255 255 255>BETA<color 135 181 255> subroutines have increased functionality and require only two memory slots.\n\n<color 255 255 255>GOLD<color 135 181 255> subroutines are fully optimized and require only one memory slot.\n\nTo optimize a subroutine, drag it onto the optimizer in the center of the subroutine screen <color 255 255 0>(Figures 1 and 2)<color 135 181 255>. Note: An optimizer may only be used once."
    8186                            "<color 135 181 255>Basics: Sec Rezzers@<color 200 241 255>Sec Rezzers\n<color 135 181 255>A Sec Rezzer is an advanced security device and are commonplace throughout the digital universe. When an illegal program is detected, nearby security programs (a.k.a Intrusion Countermeasure Programs - ICPs) will activate a Sec Rezzer to rez-in additional ICP programs for support.\n\nIt's possible to deactivate a Sec Rezzer only if you have the correct permissions.\n\nTo deactivate, face the large button on the Sec Rezzer and press the 'Action' key <color 255 255 0>(Default Key: F)<color 135 181 255>."
    8187                            "<color 135 181 255>Programs: ICP@<color 200 241 255>Intrusion Counter Measure Programs (ICPs)\n<color 135 181 255>Intrusion Countermeasure Programs (ICPs) are system programs under the command of the Kernel. They closely monitor all network traffic and will counter with force--viruses, unauthorized programs and other misuses of resources. The armor protection worn by ICP units provides a high degree of defense against attacks. However, the armor is generally ineffectual against disc base weapons."
    8188                            "<color 135 181 255>Mission 1: Exit Port@<color 200 241 255>Exit Ports\n<color 135 181 255>An Exit Port functions as a gateway to the network, allowing travel between all the components that make up the digital universe. To enter an exit port, walk directly into the beam."
    8189                            "<color 135 181 255>Basics: Light Cycles@<color 200 241 255>Default Light Cycle Controls\n<color 135 181 255>Turn left - <color 255 255 0>A<color 135 181 255>\nTurn right - <color 255 255 0>D<color 135 181 255>\nAccelerate - <color 255 255 0>W<color 135 181 255>\nDecelerate - <color 255 255 0>S<color 135 181 255>\nCamera zoom in - <color 255 255 0>Mouse wheel up<color 135 181 255>\nCamera zoom out - <color 255 255 0>Mouse wheel down<color 135 181 255>\nCamera lock toggle - <color 255 255 0>Semi-colon (;)<color 135 181 255>"
    8190                            "<color 135 181 255>Weapons: Rod Primitive@<color 200 241 255>Rod Primitive\n<color 135 181 255>A derivative of the handle-bar used to power up light cycles, the Rod Primitive, or P-rod, can also be used as a stealth base weapon. For best results, first ready the weapon, approach an enemy without being detected, then press and hold the 'Fire' key <color 255 255 0>(Default Key: Left mouse button)<color 135 181 255>."
    8191                            "<color 135 181 255>Basics: Finders@<color 200 241 255>Finders\n<color 135 181 255>Finders are patrolling security devices searching for illegal programs.  If an illegal program is detected, the Finder will notify nearby ICP guards prior to targeting the program with it's deadly laser cannon."
END

STRINGTABLE DISCARDABLE
BEGIN
    8192                            "<color 135 181 255>Encryption Keys@<color 200 241 255>Encryption Keys\n<color 135 181 255>Two Encryption Keys prevent access to the Processing platform. Disengaging the Encryption Keys without the proper permission set is impossible. However, they can be destroyed using either the Prod or Suffusion weapon."
    8193                            "<color 135 181 255>Mission 2: Mooring Apps@<color 200 241 255>Mooring Apps\n<color 135 181 255>There are three Moorings Apps keeping the transport at bay. All three Mooring Apps must be released before the transport can exit the terminal station. The control switch releasing a Mooring App can be activated only if its permission set requirement is met."
    8194                            "<color 135 181 255>Basics: Energy Pools@<color 200 241 255>Energy Pools\n<color 135 181 255>Energy pools are infinite energy sources that you may use during your missions. By standing in an energy pool, your energy will gradually replenish."
    8195                            "<color 135 181 255>Mission 2: Docking Seals@<color 200 241 255>Docking Seals\n<color 135 181 255>There are two Docking Seals impeding the evacuation of Ma3a. Both must be destroyed before she is freed from her docking structure.\n\nTo destroy a Docking Seal, approach the binary bit located in each socket and transfer energy to it until the meter displayed in the HUD fills up."
    8196                            "<color 135 181 255>Mission 3: Archive Stacks@<color 200 241 255>Archive Stacks\n<color 135 181 255>Archive Stacks are memory cells which are used to store data. Alan has three main stack partitions in his system.  Each contains a fragment of the important Tron Legacy email. Retriever programs are willing to retrieve the requested data unless the request is unauthorized."
    8197                            "<color 135 181 255>Mission 4: Power Router@<color 200 241 255>Power Router\n<color 135 181 255>Power Routers are ICP occupied nodes that control the power distribution throughout the system."
    8198                            "<color 135 181 255>Mission 4: Recognizer Tug@<color 200 241 255>Recognizer Tug\n<color 135 181 255>Recognizers were once considered start-of-the-art enforcers, regulating the will of the MCP upon the network during the early days of the mainframe. Today, due to their power and upgradeability, these lumbering crafts have been retrofitted into transport tugs, and are used to move data quickly between terminal ports."
    8199                            "<color 135 181 255>Mission 4: Communication Bits@<color 200 241 255>Communication Bits\n<color 135 181 255>Three Communication Bits monitor the many incoming power requests sent to the Power Router. Power is the life-blood and must be allocated efficiently. If one or more bit is out of operation, the Kernel is immediately notified and ICP units are sent to investigate the situation."
    8200                            "<color 135 181 255>Mission 4: Protocol Socket@<color 200 241 255>Protocol Socket\n<color 135 181 255>Ma3a can access and modify the Defense Protocols by using the protocol socket located at the base of the tower. The area around the protocol socket is heavily guarded by ICPs."
    8201                            "<color 135 181 255>Mission 4: Oculus Lenses@<color 200 241 255>Oculus Lenses\n<color 135 181 255>Four Oculus lenses are needed to properly focus the power stream from the Power Router to the desired destination. A lens can be moved into place by using the lens control panel on the main level. However, if a program lacks the required permission set needed to operate the control panel, the lenses can be manipulated by activating the Override Bits located on the lower level."
    8202                            "<color 135 181 255>Mission 4: Override Bits@<color 200 241 255>Override Bits\n<color 135 181 255>Situated on the level below the lenses, four Override Bits can manually raise and lower a lens without using the control panel located on the main level."
    8203                            "<color 135 181 255>Mission 5: Processor Gate Jumpers@<color 200 241 255>Processor Gate Jumpers\n<color 135 181 255>Although it has never been done to EN12-82, in theory, it is possible to over clock the system by flipping the four Processor Gate Jumpers. The gate jumpers are located on the top of the Processor core and can only be reached by using the center lift."
    8204                            "<color 135 181 255>Resource Hogs@<color 200 241 255>Programs: Resource Hogs\n<color 135 181 255>Over time it is common for programs to become bloated with features and upgrades to the point where their demands on the system reach debilitating proportions. Such programs hunger for resources and will stop at nothing to acquire them."
    8205                            "<color 135 181 255>Mission 5: Relay Station@<color 200 241 255>Relay Station\n<color 135 181 255>There are four Relay Stations that divert power to the Processor Core. The two primary Relay Stations are currently active but the other two Secondary Relay Stations are sitting idle. To over clock the system, the two Secondary Relay Stations must be activated."
    8206                            "<color 135 181 255>Mission 5: Legacy Code Directory@<color 200 241 255>Legacy Code Directory\n<color 135 181 255>Once thought to be only lore, an obscure directory containing TRON legacy code has been discovered. The program TRON is well known for his part in freeing the system from the evil MCP twenty years earlier and the finding of additional TRON code is truly a historical moment for the system."
    8207                            "<color 135 181 255>Mission 5: Surge Circuits@<color 200 241 255>Surge Circuits\n<color 135 181 255>Surge Suppression Circuits protect the system from power spikes. If one or more become blocked, it becomes imperative that the power build-up be released manually. Failure to do so will result in component meltdown potentially damaging critical systems."
END

STRINGTABLE DISCARDABLE
BEGIN
    8208                            "<color 135 181 255>Mission 5: TRON Legacy Code@<color 200 241 255>TRON Legacy Code\n<color 135 181 255>Little is known about what the TRON Legacy Code is or what it can do. However, according Alan's email, it is clearly a derivative of the original TRON program."
    8209                            "<color 135 181 255>Mission 6: Security Clamp@<color 200 241 255>Security Clamp\n<color 135 181 255>A lone binary bit socket located on the lower level controls the Security Clamps locking the rings in place.\nTo access the lower lever, the missing bits that control the various data streams must first be found."
    8210                            "<color 135 181 255>Mission 7: Controller Tower@<color 200 241 255>Controller Tower\n<color 135 181 255>Thorne's server is severely riddled with infected programs and corrupted data. A single Controller Tower prevents the viral nature of his corruption from completely destroying the server, rendering it useless, even to Thorne himself.\n\nDestroying the Controller Tower will bring down the protective barrier surrounding the internal partition which directly leads to Thorne's Chamber Core.\n\nThe ICP's previous attempts to take down the tower have failed due to insufficient power. However, it is believed that multiple blasts from derezzing Rector Scripts near the tower can damage it enough to take it offline."
    8211                            "<color 135 181 255>Mission 8: PDA@<color 200 241 255>PDA\n<color 135 181 255>In the real world, Thorne, like many people, uses a PDA to help manage personal affairs. Stored on his PDA is the wireless dial-up number that can access the fCon database by directly bypassing the high security firewall. Fortunately, Thorne left the device connected to the network before his infamous demonstration of the digitizing procedure to the fCon team.\n\nExtracting the number from the OS operating the PDA will not be easy, but like most hand-held electrical devices, the battery life is relatively short and sensitive to heavy usage. Turning on all the functions of the PDA should threaten the OS into activating the dial-up number."
    8212                            "<color 135 181 255>Mission 9: Firewall Exit Port@<color 200 241 255>Firewall Exit Port\n<color 135 181 255>Controlling the frequency of the barrier protecting the exit port leading into the firewall are two Modulator Sockets. Taking them offline will cause the barrier to lower and allow access into the high security firewall."
    8213                            "<color 135 181 255>Mission 9: Modulator Socket@<color 200 241 255>Modulator Socket\n<color 135 181 255>A Modulator Socket is a self-contained hub with four Power Tabs controlling its operation. Power Tabs can be easily destroyed by a weapon and can be identified by its yellow color."
    8214                            "<color 135 181 255>Mission 9: Firewall@<color 200 241 255>Firewall\n<color 135 181 255>A Firewall protects the fCon's high security network. Penetrating the firewall from the outside is impossible. However, it is possible to manipulate the security rings from the inside, allowing an unauthorized program or user to enter the system."
    8215                            "<color 135 181 255>Mission 9: Stabilizing Bits@<color 200 241 255>Stabilizing Bits\n<color 135 181 255>Stabilizing Bits are used to keep the massive Mobile Server from crashing. Removing them will prevent the vessel from righting itself in the event of a sudden glitch. The bits can only be ejected from the Mobile Server's onboard Command Module. However, before that can be done; the clamps holding them in place must first be destroyed."
    8216                            "<color 135 181 255>Mission 10: Generator Room@<color 200 241 255>Generator Room\n<color 135 181 255>Located inside the Generator Room are various systems controlling the security functions of the Mobile Server. Taking offline individual systems will ultimately grant access to even the most secure areas of the craft, namely the Digitizing Core."
    8217                            "<color 135 181 255>Mission 10: Stabilizer Control Station@<color 200 241 255>Stabilizer Control Station\n<color 135 181 255>The four Stabilizing Bits can be ejected from their sockets by activating the bit icons located within the Mobile Server's holographic display. Once the bits have been removed, turn the navigation wheel to cause an irreversible crash sequence to begin."
    8218                            "<color 135 181 255>Weapons: Ball Primitive@<color 200 241 255>Ball Primitive\n<color 135 181 255>A Ball Primitive is a by-product of the virus and is commonly used as a weapon by corrupted programs. It's highly explosive but difficult to control due to its erratic behavior. To throw a ball, press and hold the 'Fire' key <color 255 255 0>(Default Key: Left mouse button)<color 135 181 255> then release. The longer the fire key is held down, the farther the ball will travel."
    8219                            "<color 135 181 255>Basics: Performance Ratings@<color 200 241 255>Performance Ratings\n<color 135 181 255>To increase or decrease your performance ratings, click on the arrows <color 255 255 0>(Figure 1)<color 135 181 255>. The light blue meters <color 255 255 0>(Figure 2)<color 135 181 255> indicate the amount of performance increase still available. Surging <color 255 255 0>(Figure 3)<color 135 181 255> a performance rating will give you a significant boost in performance.\n\n<color 255 255 255>HEALTH <color 135 181 255>boosts your health beyond the normal 100 points.\n<color 255 255 255>ENERGY <color 135 181 255>boosts your energy beyond the normal 100 points.\n<color 255 255 255>WEAPON EFFICIENCY <color 135 181 255>reduces the energy cost required to fire a weapon.\n<color 255 255 255>TRANSFER RATE<color 135 181 255> increases the speed at which uploads and downloads take place.\n<color 255 255 255>PROCESSOR <color 135 181 255>increases the speed of your Port, Defrag and Disinfect procedurals."
    8220                            "<color 135 181 255>Basics: Illegal Program Termination@<color 200 241 255>Illegal Program Termination\n<color 255 255 0>WARNING: Do not attack civilian system programs!<color 135 181 255>  If a civilian system program is de-rezzed, you will fail your mission due to an 'Illegal Program Termination'. When equipped with a weapon such as your disc, your reticle in the center of your screen will indicate which programs are civilians <color 255 255 0>(Figure 1)<color 135 181 255> and which  are your enemies <color 255 255 0>(Figure 2)<color 135 181 255>."
    8221                            "<color 135 181 255>Programs: Data Wraith@<color 200 241 255>DataWraith\n<color 135 181 255>DataWraiths are specially trained hackers digitized into the computer with the primary goal of infiltrating the world's computer networks from the inside."
    8222                            "<color 135 181 255>Mission 1: Quarantine Protocol@<color 200 241 255>Quarantine Protocol\n<color 135 181 255>In the event an unauthorized or corrupted program attempts to use the system's exit port, quarantine countermeasures are put into place. Without a direct order from the Kernel to stand-down, the quarantine cannot be lowered. However, it is possible to override the four control bits located in small rooms surrounding the exit port's entrance.\n\nTo override a control bit, press and hold the 'Action' key <color 255 255 0>(Default Key: F) <color 135 181 255> until the bit is destroyed (also indicated when the colored meter on the screen fills up.)"
    8223                            "<color 135 181 255>Basics: Light Cycle Powerups I@<color 200 241 255>Powerup Summary Part I\n<color 255 255 255>Shield Break (Fig. 1)<color 135 181 255> powerup allows the light cycle to break through one light cycle trail.\n\n<color 255 255 255>Turbo Boost (Fig. 2)<color 135 181 255> powerup will increase your light cycle's speed for a limited time.\n\n<color 255 255 255>Wall Spike (Fig. 3)<color 135 181 255> powerup will generate a small perpendicular intersecting trail piece behind your light cycle that other light cyclists will have to avoid.\n\n<color 255 255 255>Missile (Fig. 4)<color 135 181 255> powerup will fire off a projectile that will destroy any light cycle and/or trails in its path.\n\n<color 255 255 255>Force Use (Fig. 5)<color 135 181 255> powerup will immediately activate all opponent's powerups if they are carrying one."
END

STRINGTABLE DISCARDABLE
BEGIN
    8224                            "<color 135 181 255>Basics: Light Cycle Tips@<color 200 241 255>Light Cycle Tips\n\n<color 255 255 255>Tip 1: <color 135 181 255>The objective is to force the opponents to crash into a light cycle trail or some other solid object.\n\n<color 255 255 255>Tip 2: <color 135 181 255>Ramming your bike into an opponent's bike will give you the win.\n\n<color 255 255 255>Tip 3: <color 135 181 255>Green speed zones increase the speed of your light cycle. Red speed zones decrease the speed of your light cycle. Speed zones override Turbo and Turbo Curse powerups.\n\n<color 255 255 255>Tip 4: <color 135 181 255>Light cycle camera control can be adjusted in the Option/Game menu."
    8225                            "<color 135 181 255>Basics: Light Cycle Powerups II@<color 200 241 255>Powerup Summary Part II\n<color 255 255 255>Wall Reset (Fig. 1)<color 135 181 255> powerup will remove all existing light cycle trails.\n\n<color 255 255 255>Wall Extender (Fig. 2)<color 135 181 255> powerup will add additional length to your light cycle trail.\n\n<color 255 255 255>Grab (Fig. 3)<color 135 181 255> powerup will take a powerup from the nearest source (opponent or grid.)\n\n<color 255 255 255>Turbo Curse (Fig. 4)<color 135 181 255> powerup will give you and all other opponents an excessive speed boost for a limited time.\n\n<color 255 255 255>Random (Fig. 5)<color 135 181 255> powerup will give you a randomly selected powerup."
    8226                            "<color 135 181 255>Basics: Procedurals@<color 200 241 255>Procedurals\n<color 135 181 255>Procedurals are system programs that help you manage your subroutines.\n\n<color 255 255 255>DISINFECT<color 135 181 255> analyzes a subroutine and filters out corrupt code. To disinfect a corrupted subroutine, drag it onto the 'Disinfect Procedural' <color 255 255 0>(Figure 1)<color 135 181 255>.\n\n<color 255 255 255>DEFRAG<color 135 181 255> repairs a bad block of system memory, freeing it up for subroutine use. To remove a bad block from memory ('Defragging'), drag it onto the 'Defrag Procedural' <color 255 255 0>(Figure 2)<color 135 181 255>.\n\n<color 255 255 255>PORT<color 135 181 255> converts an incompatible subroutine into one that your system can use. To port a foreign subroutine, drag it onto the 'Port Procedural' <color 255 255 0>(Figure 3)<color 135 181 255>."
END

